# --- TrainAI artifacts -----------------------------------------------------
# Training runs, prepared datasets and exported models are large and
# machine-specific. They should never be committed.
#
# Every one of these is anchored with a leading slash, and that matters: an
# unanchored `data/` matches a directory of that name at *any* depth, which
# silently excluded the whole of src/trainai/data/ -- the entire data pipeline --
# from the repository. A clone would have installed a package that cannot import.
/runs/
/data/
/exported/
# `trainai plan` writes here by default, and it describes one machine's measured
# limits -- committing it would hand the next person a plan for hardware they do
# not have.
/plan.json
# Token shards, wherever someone points --out. Named specifically rather than
# *.bin, so the pattern cannot swallow a legitimate file the way `data/` did.
train_*.bin
val_*.bin
*.safetensors
!tests/**/*.safetensors
# Checkpoints, for the same reason and with the same care. `trainai train` writes
# `step-NNNNNN.pt` (see src/trainai/train/checkpoint.py), and a run started with
# `--out mymodel` rather than under runs/ lands them somewhere nothing else here
# covers -- which is how a repository acquires a 500 MB accident. `*.pt` would be
# the broad version of this pattern, and it is not used: it would also match a
# fixture or a file someone genuinely wants tracked.
step-*.pt
wandb/

# The product owner's original brief (kept locally, not part of the repo).
plan.md.txt

# Working copies of documents kept for reference while rewriting them -- notably
# the 887-line README this one replaced, which is preserved locally as
# reference/README-original.md. Anchored, and a directory rather than a name
# pattern, so it cannot start matching a `reference/` package inside src/. The
# content worth keeping went to docs/walkthrough.md, which is tracked: anything
# only in here is one `git clean -fdx` from being gone.
/reference/

# Datasets dropped next to the example scripts. `examples/weather_to_text.py`
# documents where its input comes from and how to fetch it; the file itself is
# 16 MB of third-party data, and a repository that carries one sample corpus
# ends up carrying five. Test fixtures live in tests/ and are unaffected.
/examples/*.csv
/examples/*.tsv
/examples/*.jsonl

# --- Python ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Unanchored on purpose: every one of these genuinely occurs at any depth in a
# source tree, so a leading slash would make them miss most of what they exist
# to catch.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
*.egg-info/
*.egg

# Everything a build backend writes -- and all of it only ever at the project
# root. Anchored for the reason given at the top of this file. `lib/` is the one
# that would hurt most: it is a single `src/trainai/lib/` away from repeating the
# incident that erased the data pipeline from the repository, and unlike `data/`
# it would not announce itself with an import failure.
/build/
/develop-eggs/
/dist/
/downloads/
/eggs/
/.eggs/
/lib/
/lib64/
/parts/
/sdist/
/var/
/wheels/
/share/python-wheels/
/.installed.cfg
/MANIFEST

# --- Virtual environments --------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
ENV/
env/

# --- Testing / coverage ----------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.tox/
.nox/
.hypothesis/

# --- Secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing in this project reads an environment file today, and that is exactly
# when to add this: the rule has to already be here on the day someone adds a
# token for `trainai export --push` or a Weights & Biases key. A secret is the
# one kind of accident `git rm --cached` does not undo, because by then it has
# been fetched, cached and indexed by whoever was watching the repository.
.env
.env.*
# An *example* file is documentation, carries no real values, and is the thing
# that keeps the rule above from being mistaken for "this project has no config".
!.env.example
# Broader than this file's usual rule that a pattern must name what it catches.
# That is deliberate here and only here: for every other artifact the cost of a
# missed match is a large file in a commit, which is annoying and reversible. For
# a private key it is a key that has to be rotated.
*.pem
*.key

# --- Editors / OS / local tool state ---------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
# Per-machine agent settings: paths and tool permissions for one checkout, of no
# use to anyone else. Listed here because it was only ever ignored by this
# machine's *global* excludes file, which no clone and no CI runner has -- so on
# any other machine it was one `git add .` from being committed.
.claude/

# --- Merge and rebase leftovers --------------------------------------------
# `git` writes these itself during a conflict. Committing one is how a repository
# ends up shipping a file with `<<<<<<<` in it.
*.orig
*.rej

# --- Jupyter ---------------------------------------------------------------
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Exported models (generated artifacts, sometimes hundreds of MB)
/models/
